          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of David Hume</strong><br>\nDavid Hume was born on May 7, 1711, in Edinburgh, Scotland. As the second son of a minor Scottish nobleman, his childhood was comfortable but not luxurious. He attended the University of Edinburgh at the young age of twelve, which was common in his time. However, he found his education there unsatisfying and left without obtaining a degree to pursue his own curriculum of learning in private study.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hume's Philosophical Beginnings</strong><br>\nIn his early twenties, Hume began his lifelong pursuit of refining his philosophical outlook. By immersing himself in the works of Cicero, Descartes, and Locke, he developed a skepticism of rationalism and religious doctrines. This skepticism became the cornerstone of his later work, as he sought to understand human nature through experience and observation rather than through religious or philosophical doctrine.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literary and Philosophical Essays</strong><br>\nAfter working on his philosophy, Hume published his first work, \"A Treatise of Human Nature,\" at the age of twenty-eight. The work received a lukewarm reception, which disappointed Hume. He later reworked his ideas into the more popular \"Essays, Moral and Political.\" His essays discussed topics from economics to religion, showcasing his range of interests and the breadth of his thought.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
