          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Idea of Empiricism</strong><br>\nHume was a key figure in the Scottish Enlightenment and a proponent of empiricism. He argued that all human knowledge arises from sensory experience and that all ideas are derived from impressions. These revolutionary ideas challenged the philosophical community and influenced the development of modern empiricism, shaping the study of human knowledge.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cause and Effect</strong><br>\nOne of Hume's most significant contributions to philosophy was his skepticism about causality. He argued that while we may observe that one event often follows another, we cannot therefore say with certainty that one causes the other. His questioning of cause and effect has had a profound impact on the philosophy of science and the methodology of scientific inquiry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Religion and Superstition</strong><br>\nHume was critical of religion and superstitious beliefs. He maintained that a rational assessment of the evidence does not support the existence of miracles or divine intervention. His writings on religion stirred controversy, but they also contributed to the secularization of philosophical discourse.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
