          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Theory and Economics</strong><br>\nApart from philosophy, Hume also wrote extensively on politics and economics. He was one of the first philosophers to discuss the idea of the balance of trade and to explain how trade can benefit a country. He believed in free trade and opposed mercantilist policies, which he felt were based on incorrect assumptions about wealth and trade.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hume's Later Years</strong><br>\nHume gained recognition in his later years, becoming a notable figure in British intellectual society. He took up various diplomatic posts in his career, serving in Vienna and Turin. His later works included \"The History of England,\" which became a bestseller and cemented his status as one of the most important thinkers of his time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of David Hume</strong><br>\nDavid Hume died on August 25, 1776, leaving behind a legacy that would influence many areas of philosophy, economics, and political theory. His empirical approach to human understanding laid the groundwork for later philosophers and economists. Hume's works continue to be studied for their clear-eyed and rational approach to complex questions about human nature and the workings of society.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
